<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!-- No engine-side ceiling: a query returns what it asks for.

     THIS FILE IS LOAD-BEARING, not a tuning knob. Every field below is one the
     engine refuses to take from a request (`maxHits`/`maxOffset` fail with
     "must be specified in a query profile"; so does `grouping.globalMaxGroups`),
     so a query profile is the ONLY place they can be set — a deployment that
     replaces this file must carry every field over.

     maxHits/maxOffset: Vespa's own defaults are 400 and 1000, and a query
     asking for more is REJECTED (`N hits requested, configured limit: 400`),
     not trimmed. Int.MAX_VALUE is how you say "don't cap"; the only bound left
     is the caller's own `limit` (and the host's memory).

     grouping.globalMaxGroups: while this ceiling is on, Vespa fails any
     grouping pipeline that has no max() outright ("Cannot return unbounded
     number of groups"). Every aggregation this store issues — count, distinct
     count, kind histogram, distinct authors — is deliberately max()-less so it
     answers over the whole match set, so the ceiling has to be off. -1 is the
     documented "disabled" value; see EventYql.grouping, which disables the two
     per-request ceilings (defaultMaxGroups/defaultMaxHits) that DO travel as
     query parameters.

     Deciding a policy cap is the OPERATOR's call, not the library's: lower
     these if you want the engine to refuse expensive queries — but note that
     lowering globalMaxGroups breaks the aggregation paths above. A full-corpus
     dump should use a document/v1 visit (VespaEventIndex.visitIds) rather than
     a huge search — it streams instead of materializing. -->
<query-profile id="default">
    <field name="maxHits">2147483647</field>
    <field name="maxOffset">2147483647</field>
    <field name="grouping.globalMaxGroups">-1</field>
</query-profile>
